2026-07-17 09:42:21,492 - agent_何敏 - INFO - [FINALIZE CONTACT][year=2020 week=1 person=何敏] [{'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Summary of the Contact Stage:  \n\nI sent the same message to Zhao Min three times—across all three slots—because I couldn’t make myself change it. “Zhao Min, can we eat lunch together tomorrow? I want to talk… about what’s been happening in class lately.”  \n\nNot “something feels off.” Not “I heard something.” Just that. Soft. Open-ended. Like handing someone a cup of tea with both hands—not pushing, just holding it out.  \n\nShe didn’t reply.  \n\nNot once.  \n\nThat doesn’t surprise me—not really. Zhao Min isn’t careless. If she’d seen my message and chosen not to answer, it would mean she’s weighing something. But more likely, she didn’t see them. Or did—and froze, just like I did before hitting “send” the first time. Because saying *that* out loud, even in text, is like stepping off a curb you didn’t know was there.  \n\nI kept the wording the same each time because changing it felt like retreating—like adding “if you’re free” or “no pressure” would shrink the weight of the thing until it disappeared. And I don’t want it to disappear. Not yet.  \n\nNo joint activities were scheduled. No invitations came my way. No cancellations either. Just silence—not cold, not hostile, but thick, like air right before rain. The kind where everyone’s listening, but no one’s sure whether to speak first.  \n\nI’m not disappointed. Not angry. Just… quieter than before. My fingers traced the edge of my notebook while waiting for replies, thumb pressing into the spine until it left a faint white line. That’s how I hold things when I’m trying not to let go: gently, but hard enough to leave a mark.  \n\nWhat matters isn’t whether she answered—it’s that I asked.  \nAnd that I meant it.  \n\n(soft exhale, shoulders lowering just slightly)  \nThe cup is still held out.  \nIt’s okay if it stays that way—for now.'}]
